  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 AM MDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nThe H5 trough continues to exit to the southeast, and is\ngradually taking the clouds and precip with it. As of current\nwriting time, the only remaining light rain is confined to\nNatrona County, and continues to draw to the southeast as well.\nMost light rain should be complete by 6AM, but light sprinkles\nmay continue on Casper Mountain for a couple more hours. Skies\nwill clear through the rest of the day, and with good cold air\nadvection still in place, highs will struggle to reach the 50s.\nThis will be accompanied by a modest northerly breeze, making\nfor a brisk autumn day. Areas across Johnson County will hold\nsome more of the influence of the sfc low gradient, so winds\nwill be higher through the day, gusting 30 to 40 mph through the\nafternoon. Watch for cross winds along the I-90 corridor. Winds\nwill die off quickly after sunset as high pressure sweeps in.\nThis sets the stage for the first freeze of the season for most\nof the state. Most areas will fall into the 20s for several\nhours tonight. The only inhibiting factor may be enough of a\nbreeze in the Wind River Basin to mix things enough to limit the\noverall timing of the freeze, but this airmass is cold enough to\novercome most of this. Freeze Warnings are in effect tonight \nacross central Wyoming, where freezing temperatures have not \nyet been reached.\n\nThe main pattern this weekend is a broad H5 ridge across the \nRockies, bringing moderating temperatures and deflecting the \nstorm track back into southern Canada. The main impact will be\nwhich way the winds are from. Winds Saturday will be from the\nsouthwest, bringing the warmer air back into the region. Sunday\na weather system passes across southern Canada, switching winds\nback from the north. While no cold air will return with these\nwinds, it will be enough to bring temperatures back to near\nnormal around 60 degrees. The next weather impact across western\nWyoming still looking to be sometime Tuesday, when the ridge\nis broken down by a trough along the Rockies, bringing more\nmoisture back from the southwest.",
